---CLI---
     1	---
     2	title: Commands
     3	description: Create custom commands for repetitive tasks.
     4	---
     5	
     6	Custom commands let you specify a prompt you want to run when that command is executed in the TUI.
     7	
     8	```bash frame="none"
     9	/my-command
    10	```
    11	
    12	Custom commands are in addition to the built-in commands like `/init`, `/undo`, `/redo`, `/share`, `/help`. [Learn more](/docs/tui#commands).
    13	
    14	---
    15	
    16	## Create command files
    17	
    18	Create markdown files in the `commands/` directory to define custom commands.
    19	
    20	Create `.opencode/commands/test.md`:
    21	
    22	```md title=".opencode/commands/test.md"
    23	---
    24	description: Run tests with coverage
    25	agent: build
    26	model: anthropic/claude-3-5-sonnet-20241022
    27	---
    28	
    29	Run the full test suite with coverage report and show any failures.
    30	Focus on the failing tests and suggest fixes.
    31	```
    32	
    33	The frontmatter defines command properties. The content becomes the template.
    34	
    35	Use the command by typing `/` followed by the command name.
    36	
    37	```bash frame="none"
    38	"/test"
    39	```
    40	
    41	---
    42	
    43	## Configure
    44	
    45	You can add custom commands through the OpenCode config or by creating markdown files in the `commands/` directory.
    46	
    47	---
    48	
    49	### JSON
    50	
    51	Use the `command` option in your OpenCode [config](/docs/config):
    52	
    53	```json title="opencode.jsonc" {4-12}
    54	{
    55	  "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/config.json",
    56	  "command": {
    57	    // This becomes the name of the command
    58	    "test": {
    59	      // This is the prompt that will be sent to the LLM
    60	      "template": "Run the full test suite with coverage report and show any failures.\nFocus on the failing tests and suggest fixes.",
    61	      // This is shown as the description in the TUI
    62	      "description": "Run tests with coverage",
    63	      "agent": "build",
    64	      "model": "anthropic/claude-3-5-sonnet-20241022"
    65	    }
    66	  }
    67	}
    68	```
    69	
    70	Now you can run this command in the TUI:
    71	
    72	```bash frame="none"
    73	/test
    74	```
    75	
    76	---
    77	
    78	### Markdown
    79	
    80	You can also define commands using markdown files. Place them in:
    81	
    82	- Global: `~/.config/opencode/commands/`
    83	- Per-project: `.opencode/commands/`
    84	
    85	```markdown title="~/.config/opencode/commands/test.md"
    86	---
    87	description: Run tests with coverage
    88	agent: build
    89	model: anthropic/claude-3-5-sonnet-20241022
    90	---
    91	
    92	Run the full test suite with coverage report and show any failures.
    93	Focus on the failing tests and suggest fixes.
    94	```
    95	
    96	The markdown file name becomes the command name. For example, `test.md` lets
    97	you run:
    98	
    99	```bash frame="none"
   100	/test
   101	```
   102	
   103	---
   104	
   105	## Prompt config
   106	
   107	The prompts for the custom commands support several special placeholders and syntax.
   108	
   109	---
   110	
   111	### Arguments
   112	
   113	Pass arguments to commands using the `$ARGUMENTS` placeholder.
   114	
   115	```md title=".opencode/commands/component.md"
   116	---
   117	description: Create a new component
   118	---
   119	
   120	Create a new React component named $ARGUMENTS with TypeScript support.

---SKILLS---
     1	---
     2	title: "Agent Skills"
     3	description: "Define reusable behavior via SKILL.md definitions"
     4	---
     5	
     6	Agent skills let OpenCode discover reusable instructions from your repo or home directory.
     7	Skills are loaded on-demand via the native `skill` tool—agents see available skills and can load the full content when needed.
     8	
     9	---
    10	
    11	## Place files
    12	
    13	Create one folder per skill name and put a `SKILL.md` inside it.
    14	OpenCode searches these locations:
    15	
    16	- Project config: `.opencode/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`
    17	- Global config: `~/.config/opencode/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`
    18	- Project Claude-compatible: `.claude/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`
    19	- Global Claude-compatible: `~/.claude/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`
    20	- Project agent-compatible: `.agents/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`
    21	- Global agent-compatible: `~/.agents/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`
    22	
    23	---
    24	
    25	## Understand discovery
    26	
    27	For project-local paths, OpenCode walks up from your current working directory until it reaches the git worktree.
    28	It loads any matching `skills/*/SKILL.md` in `.opencode/` and any matching `.claude/skills/*/SKILL.md` or `.agents/skills/*/SKILL.md` along the way.
    29	
    30	Global definitions are also loaded from `~/.config/opencode/skills/*/SKILL.md`, `~/.claude/skills/*/SKILL.md`, and `~/.agents/skills/*/SKILL.md`.
    31	
    32	---
    33	
    34	## Write frontmatter
    35	
    36	Each `SKILL.md` must start with YAML frontmatter.
    37	Only these fields are recognized:
    38	
    39	- `name` (required)
    40	- `description` (required)
    41	- `license` (optional)
    42	- `compatibility` (optional)
    43	- `metadata` (optional, string-to-string map)
    44	
    45	Unknown frontmatter fields are ignored.
    46	
    47	---
    48	
    49	## Validate names
    50	
    51	`name` must:
    52	
    53	- Be 1–64 characters
    54	- Be lowercase alphanumeric with single hyphen separators
    55	- Not start or end with `-`
    56	- Not contain consecutive `--`
    57	- Match the directory name that contains `SKILL.md`
    58	
    59	Equivalent regex:
    60	
    61	```text
    62	^[a-z0-9]+(-[a-z0-9]+)*$
    63	```
    64	
    65	---
    66	
    67	## Follow length rules
    68	
    69	`description` must be 1-1024 characters.
    70	Keep it specific enough for the agent to choose correctly.
    71	
    72	---
    73	
    74	## Use an example
    75	
    76	Create `.opencode/skills/git-release/SKILL.md` like this:
    77	
    78	```markdown
    79	---
    80	name: git-release
    81	description: Create consistent releases and changelogs
    82	license: MIT
    83	compatibility: opencode
    84	metadata:
    85	  audience: maintainers
    86	  workflow: github
    87	---
    88	
    89	## What I do
    90	
    91	- Draft release notes from merged PRs
    92	- Propose a version bump
    93	- Provide a copy-pasteable `gh release create` command
    94	
    95	## When to use me
    96	
    97	Use this when you are preparing a tagged release.
    98	Ask clarifying questions if the target versioning scheme is unclear.
    99	```
   100	
   101	---
   102	
   103	## Recognize tool description
   104	
   105	OpenCode lists available skills in the `skill` tool description.
   106	Each entry includes the skill name and description:
   107	
   108	```xml
   109	<available_skills>
   110	  <skill>
   111	    <name>git-release</name>
   112	    <description>Create consistent releases and changelogs</description>
   113	  </skill>
   114	</available_skills>
   115	```
   116	
   117	The agent loads a skill by calling the tool:
   118	
   119	```
   120	skill({ name: "git-release" })
   121	```
   122	
   123	---
   124	
   125	## Configure permissions
   126	
   127	Control which skills agents can access using pattern-based permissions in `opencode.json`:
   128	
   129	```json
   130	{
   131	  "permission": {
   132	    "skill": {
   133	      "*": "allow",
   134	      "pr-review": "allow",
   135	      "internal-*": "deny",
   136	      "experimental-*": "ask"
   137	    }
   138	  }
   139	}
   140	```

